This is the inside front cover (pastedown) of a small notebook. The facing page carries the Latin diary-style entries of May–October 1800 on lemniscatic functions, the arithmetic-geometric mean, elliptic transcendents and a continued-fraction probability problem. The cover is a scratch surface from Gauss's Braunschweig years, c. 1797–1808. It holds almost no mathematics.

(1) Walking or road-measurement notes. Clock times are paired with running counts, very likely paces:
- 5.14 last stone in the Chaussee at the Steinthor;
- '106½' (perhaps a pace rate) on the 7th near a Carrière/inn, count 3100;
- 5.41 at a tower (sketch), count 4522;
- 6h 8' at the stone at the Fallersleben gate, count 5982.
From 4522 to 5982 is 1460 units in 27 min, about 54 units/min. The unit and starting point are uncertain (my inference).

(2) A road itinerary along the Chaussee from Braunschweig to Helmstedt, in the same units:
- end of Chaussee 0;
- Hoppenstedt(?) tower 5470;
- Kl. Schöppenstedt 8300;
- Cremlingen 11370;
- Abbenrode 18320;
- Bornum 21520;
- Königslutter 26700;
- Süpplingen 36000;
- Helmstedt 46000.
The successive differences are 5470, 2830, 3070, 6950, 3200, 5180, 9300, 10000. About 46000 units for the roughly 35–37 km road fits a pace of about 0.75–0.8 m (my inference).

(3) A boxed journey of 6 April 1801 from Wolfenbüttel to Braunschweig, timed in minutes:
- inner Wolfenbüttel gate 0';
- outer gate 3½;
- (…) bridge 20;
- Braunschweig(?) 26½;
- August(?) gate toll 39½;
- inner (gate) 42¾.

(4) An expense or trip list: Göttingen, Helmstedt, Kassel, Harz, Heiligenstadt, Rosenthal(?), Einbeck(?), Moringen, … The totals line reads 132 . 56 . 40. The first column checks: 72+10+10+30+6+4 = 132. A money note reads '10 Rt 14 ggr 5 S' (Reichsthaler, gute Groschen, Pfennig/Schilling).

(5) Literary and personal notes:
- Pope's epitaph on Newton, 'Nature and Nature's laws lay hid in night; God said, Let Newton be! and all was light';
- the names Bohnstedt, Baumannshöhle (the Harz cave), 'Dr W…', and 'Friedrich etc'.

(6) Astronomical scraps:
- 'Oppos' (opposition), with the number 2,57381;
- clock times 3h 2m 10s, 3h 1m 12s, 3h 1' 2'', 6h 20', 44'…45';
- 'Handel 7000–7500';
- 6,57 and 6,1;
- the symbol ϖπ/ϖ'.
ϖ is Gauss's lemniscatic half-period 2.62205755…. He knew (1799) that π/ϖ = M(√2,1), the arithmetic-geometric mean. ϖ' is unexplained. These are bare tokens with no derivation.

(7) A year table 1784–1808 with counts:
- 1784: 6;
- 1795: 17;
- 1796: 45;
- 1797: 79 (16);
- 1798: 63 (14);
- 1799: 29 (10);
- 1800: 15 (10);
- 1803: 97 (4, 93);
- 1804: 15 (12);
- 1805: 4;
- 1808: 51 (8).
Its meaning is unclear: possibly books bought or borrowed, letters, or similar counts. It does not obviously match the numbers of diary entries per year.

(8) Loose arithmetic at upper left: 3057, 1019, 1133, 1518½, 1466, 2020. Note that 3057 = 3·1019.

No derivation, theorem or method is present.
